\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{The law on a selected subspace}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{For a [selected background](fdip-005A), view #{P} as a map
#{H\to X=\operatorname{im}P} and let #{r=\dim X}. The law
#{\nu_b=P_*\mu_Q} is the centered Gaussian on #{X} with intrinsic
covariance and precision
##{C_P=(PGP)|_X>0,\qquad S_P=C_P^{-1}:X\to X.}
Relative to the induced Euclidean volume #{\lambda_X}, its density is
##{\frac{d\nu_b}{d\lambda_X}(x)
 =\frac{\sqrt{\det_X S_P}}{(2\pi)^{r/2}}
   e^{-\langle x,S_Px\rangle /2}.}
If #{B:\mathbb R^r\to H} is an isometric inclusion with image #{X},
then #{BB^*=P}; its chosen coordinate matrices are
##{B^*C_PB=B^*GB,\qquad [S_P]_B=(B^*GB)^{-1}.}
For another such basis #{B'=BU}, #{U\in O(r)}, the latter changes
to #{U^*[S_P]_BU}. The intrinsic operator #{S_P} is independent
of this choice.}

\proof{
\p{For nonzero #{x\in X},
#{\langle x,C_Px\rangle=\langle x,Gx\rangle>0}.
Set #{C=B^*GB}, so #{C>0}. The positive square roots of #{Q}
and #{C} exist by the real spectral theorem. The density
change of variables #{z=Q^{1/2}\phi} gives the standard Gaussian
density #{(2\pi)^{-|V|/2}e^{-|z|^2/2}}: its Jacobian and the
[normalizer](fdip-002T) cancel. Put
##{T=C^{-1/2}B^*Q^{-1/2}.}
Then #{TT^*=C^{-1/2}(B^*GB)C^{-1/2}=I_r}, so its rows extend
to an orthonormal basis of #{H}. The resulting square orthogonal
matrix carries #{z} to a standard Gaussian, since its Jacobian
has absolute value one and it preserves the squared norm.
Its first #{r} coordinates are #{Tz}; integration over the other
coordinates gives the standard #{r}-dimensional density. Hence
##{B^*\phi=C^{1/2}Tz}
has density #{(2\pi)^{-r/2}(\det C)^{-1/2}
e^{-y^TC^{-1}y/2}} by an invertible change of variables.
The isometry #{B} carries Euclidean volume to #{\lambda_X} and
#{B(B^*\phi)=P\phi}. This proves the law and its density, rather
than inferring a law from its covariance alone.}

\p{For #{x,x'\in X}, the covariance of the corresponding linear
observations is #{\langle x,Gx'\rangle=\langle x,C_Px'\rangle}
by the [inverse-precision formula](fdip-000B). The inverse identity
and #{B'=BU} give the coordinate statements.}
}

\p{In the orthogonal split #{H=X\oplus Y}, write
##{Q=\begin{pmatrix}A&K\\K^*&D\end{pmatrix},\qquad D>0.}
Then
##{S_P=A-KD^{-1}K^*.}
Indeed, solving #{Q(x,y)=(u,0)} gives
#{y=-D^{-1}K^*x} and #{(A-KD^{-1}K^*)x=u}.
Positivity follows by evaluating the quadratic form on
#{(x,-D^{-1}K^*x)}. Thus the #{X,X} block of #{Q^{-1}}
is the inverse of this Schur complement, and it is exactly #{C_P}.
For #{P=P_R}, this recovers the [coordinate marginal](fdip-000E).
If #{PQ=QP}, then #{K=0} and #{S_P=Q|_X}; symmetry of a
subspace alone does not justify that equality.}

\p{\citet{Appendix A.2, p. 200}{rasmussen2006gaussian}
gives the covariance-density and marginal conventions in (A.4)-(A.6).
\citet{Definition 8.3, p. 384}{friedli2017statistical}
characterizes Gaussian vectors by linear observations.}

\p{\citet{Theorem 8.4, p. 385}{friedli2017statistical}
gives their nonsingular density. The row-isometry argument above supplies the
linear-image step, including its Euclidean-volume identification.}

\p{A partial formal correspondence is supplied by TauCeti's
\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.Probability.map_affine_multivariateGaussian}.
It allows finite real coordinate spaces, a positive semidefinite
covariance and an arbitrary rectangular linear map. Set its mean
and shift to zero, its covariance to #{G=Q^{-1}>0}, and its matrix
to #{B^*}. After the [precision-density identification](fdip-000B),
the result gives the coordinate image law with covariance #{B^*GB}.
It does not require full row rank. Positivity of this compressed
covariance, its inverse precision, the isometric identification
with #{X}, and the graph data are the additional arguments above.}
